WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.116
This is Read Tabs, Play Guitar, by Fretbox. Guitar tab and notation, one step at a time.

00:00:07.916 --> 00:00:11.316
Today: tab symbols and standard notation for guitar.

00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:17.473
Tab symbols. Slides, bends, hammer-ons and more.

00:00:18.273 --> 00:00:34.501
Guitarists do more than pick single notes. Tab uses letters and symbols for the techniques that make the guitar sing. The symbols below are the most common ones. Different websites and books sometimes use slightly different conventions, so always check the legend if a tab has one.

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:37.423
Hammer-on and pull-off.

00:00:38.223 --> 00:00:50.292
h, hammer-on. Pick the first note, then bring another finger down hard on a higher fret, without picking again. 5 h 7 means: pick fret 5, hammer on to fret 7.

00:00:51.092 --> 00:01:01.022
p, pull-off. The opposite: from a higher fret, pull your finger off sideways so the lower note sounds, without picking again. 7 p 5.

00:01:01.822 --> 00:01:03.813
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:06.104
Slides.

00:01:06.904 --> 00:01:13.262
/ slide up: 5 / 7 means play fret 5 and slide up to fret 7 without lifting the finger.

00:01:14.062 --> 00:01:17.283
\ slide down: 7 \ 5.

00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:19.920
Bends and vibrato.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:30.711
b, bend. Push the string sideways to raise its pitch. 7 b 9 means: fret 7, bent until it sounds like fret 9 (a whole tone up).

00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:36.778
r, release. Let a bent string come back down: 7 b 9 r 7.

00:01:37.578 --> 00:01:41.335
~, vibrato. Shake the note slightly to make it wobble in pitch.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.190
Other common symbols.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.756
x: a muted, percussive note, with no clear pitch.

00:01:49.556 --> 00:01:54.698
PM: palm muting; rest the side of your picking hand lightly on the strings near the bridge.

00:01:55.498 --> 00:02:01.724
<12>: a natural harmonic; touch the string lightly above fret 12, without pressing, and pick.

00:02:02.524 --> 00:02:12.112
Tip: Learn each technique slowly, on its own, before you use it in a song. A clean hammer-on at slow speed is the foundation of fast legato playing later.

00:02:13.612 --> 00:02:14.333
Practice.

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:21.658
First: play 5 h 7 and 7 p 5 on string 3 until both notes sound clearly and evenly.

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:27.397
Second: slide from fret 5 to fret 7 on string 2, then back down.

00:02:28.197 --> 00:02:36.199
Third: bend fret 7 on string 3 until it matches fret 9. Use a tuner, or play fret 9 first to hear the target.

00:02:36.999 --> 00:02:41.305
Fourth: try the natural harmonics at fret 12 on all six strings.

00:02:43.105 --> 00:02:46.670
Standard notation for guitar. The staff above the tab.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:03:06.310
Many guitar books and most tab files show two systems together: standard notation on top and tab underneath. Tab tells you where to play; notation tells you which notes and, above all, how long they last. You do not need to read notation fluently to benefit from it. Knowing a few basics already helps.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.062
The staff and the clef.

00:03:09.862 --> 00:03:20.202
Notation is written on a staff of five lines. Guitar music uses the treble clef, the curly sign at the start of each line. Notes higher on the staff sound higher.

00:03:21.002 --> 00:03:37.125
One detail surprises people: guitar music is written one octave higher than it sounds. The guitar sounds one octave lower than written. This keeps the notes on the staff instead of far below it. You do not need to do anything about it; it is simply how guitar music is written.

00:03:38.625 --> 00:03:39.523
Note lengths.

00:03:40.323 --> 00:03:44.641
The shape of a note tells you how long it lasts. In 4/4 time:

00:03:45.441 --> 00:03:48.655
A whole note (an open oval) lasts four beats.

00:03:49.455 --> 00:03:53.264
A half note (an open oval with a stem) lasts two beats.

00:03:54.064 --> 00:03:57.706
A quarter note (a filled oval with a stem) lasts one beat.

00:03:58.506 --> 00:04:04.753
An eighth note (a filled oval with a stem and a flag, or joined to others by a beam) lasts half a beat.

00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:10.016
A sixteenth note (two flags or two beams) lasts a quarter of a beat.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:15.455
Each note has a matching rest, a symbol that means silence for the same length of time.

00:04:16.955 --> 00:04:18.073
Time signatures.

00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:35.863
The two numbers at the start of a piece are the time signature. The top number tells you how many beats there are in each bar; the bottom number tells you which note gets one beat. In 4/4, the most common, there are four quarter-note beats per bar. In 3/4 (a waltz), there are three.

00:04:36.663 --> 00:04:45.492
Tip: If a tab has no rhythm, and the song is unfamiliar, the notation above it is where the rhythm is. Clap the rhythm first, then play it.

00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:47.713
Practice.

00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:55.595
First: find a tab file with notation. Clap the rhythm of the first two bars while counting one-and-two-and-three-and-four-and.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:05:00.871
Second: play the same two bars on the guitar, keeping the rhythm you clapped.

00:05:01.671 --> 00:05:07.777
Third: look at the time signature of five songs you like. Which are in 4/4, and which are not?

00:05:09.577 --> 00:05:10.638
That's all for today.

00:05:11.438 --> 00:05:12.640
If you want to keep going:

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:24.853
Fretbox is the app behind the ebook. It is a tab player for guitarists: it opens tablature files in the Guitar Pro and MusicXML formats, so you can read a song and hear it at the same time.

00:05:25.653 --> 00:05:28.290
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be used with it:

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.850
reading tablature and the symbols for each technique.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.249
following the rhythm in the notation that comes with the tab.

00:05:36.049 --> 00:05:38.780
learning the fretboard, chords and scales.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.894
practising song by song, section by section, at your own pace.

00:05:44.694 --> 00:05:48.839
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who is starting out on the guitar.

00:05:49.639 --> 00:05:54.200
This episode of Read Tabs, Play Guitar was narrated with a synthetic voice.

